<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Czech Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1993 CE – present · capital Prague</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A landlocked parliamentary republic in Central Europe, independent since 1993 after the peaceful split of Czechoslovakia, and a NATO and EU member.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Czech Republic became independent on 1 January 1993, after <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> was peacefully dissolved on 31 December 1992 into the Czech Republic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. The split followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velvet-revolution">Velvet Revolution</a> of November 1989, which had ended communist rule, and the strengthening of Slovak national aspirations.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Both successor states carried out economic reforms and privatisations aimed at a market economy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaclav-havel">Václav Havel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaclav-klaus">Václav Klaus</a> were each elected president twice by parliament between 1993 and 2012; since 2013 the president has been elected directly. Until 2017 either the Czech Social Democratic Party or the Civic Democratic Party led the government. The country joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oecd">OECD</a> in 1995, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 1999, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 2004 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schengen-area">Schengen Area</a> in 2007. About 75% to 79% of residents declare no religion in surveys.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>In October 2017 the movement ANO 2011, led by Andrej Babiš, won the elections, and President Miloš Zeman appointed Babiš prime minister in December. Petr Fiala became prime minister after the 2021 elections, and Petr Pavel won the presidency in January 2023. After the Russian invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in 2022 the country took in around half a million Ukrainian refugees. Babiš returned as prime minister in December 2025.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/czech-republic.jpg" alt="Czech Republic" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Jaroslav Kursa · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Czech_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1993 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Prague</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Václav Havel, Václav Klaus, Miloš Zeman, Petr Pavel, Andrej Babiš</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>no religion (most residents), Roman Catholic, Protestant</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Czech</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Czechia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2010&amp;focus=czech-republic" aria-label="Czech Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Czech Republic lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5783 5758L5783 5758"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5783 5758h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5783 5758h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5783 5758h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Prague. <a href="/atlas?year=2010&amp;focus=czech-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
